1
00:00:00,862 --> 00:00:02,807
This episode does three things.

2
00:00:03,549 --> 00:00:06,032
First, it explains the Kaleido paper.

3
00:00:06,774 --> 00:00:08,437
How do you look at tens of thousands of

4
00:00:08,437 --> 00:00:10,524
neurons at the same time, and how do you

5
00:00:10,524 --> 00:00:12,087
make neighboring neurons wear different

6
00:00:12,087 --> 00:00:15,462
colors. Second, it lets the AI write the

7
00:00:15,462 --> 00:00:17,537
code from nothing, all the way to three

8
00:00:17,537 --> 00:00:19,657
views and a rotating animation.

9
00:00:20,399 --> 00:00:22,574
Third, we look back at that whole stretch

10
00:00:22,574 --> 00:00:23,849
and talk about what agentic

11
00:00:23,849 --> 00:00:25,307
coding actually is.

12
00:00:26,681 --> 00:00:28,669
The first four episodes built a knowledge

13
00:00:28,669 --> 00:00:30,831
base, wrote the rules, grew the topic

14
00:00:30,831 --> 00:00:33,156
pages, and turned a topic page into a

15
00:00:33,156 --> 00:00:36,181
lesson. This episode uses the knowledge

16
00:00:36,181 --> 00:00:39,394
base differently. You take one paper entry

17
00:00:39,394 --> 00:00:40,956
out of it, treat that entry as a

18
00:00:40,956 --> 00:00:43,369
specification, and reproduce the paper's

19
00:00:43,369 --> 00:00:45,869
code. The data is one hundred

20
00:00:45,869 --> 00:00:47,356
single-neuron images picked at

21
00:00:47,356 --> 00:00:48,789
random from FlyCircuit.

22
00:00:50,173 --> 00:00:52,180
Kaleido came out in twenty eighteen.

23
00:00:52,923 --> 00:00:54,885
It is the first tool paper in this line of

24
00:00:54,885 --> 00:00:57,905
work. The problem it solves is this.

25
00:00:58,648 --> 00:01:00,310
The earlier papers had piled up

26
00:01:00,310 --> 00:01:02,210
single-neuron images on the order of a

27
00:01:02,210 --> 00:01:04,235
hundred thousand, but seeing tens of

28
00:01:04,235 --> 00:01:06,180
thousands of them at once was stuck.

29
00:01:06,923 --> 00:01:09,105
Each image is about a hundred megabytes.

30
00:01:09,848 --> 00:01:11,460
Load all hundred and thirty thousand

31
00:01:11,460 --> 00:01:13,435
neurons of a fruit fly brain and you need

32
00:01:13,435 --> 00:01:14,980
ten terabytes of memory.

33
00:01:15,723 --> 00:01:17,255
And even if you could load them,

34
00:01:17,310 --> 00:01:19,135
off-the-shelf software has a ceiling on

35
00:01:19,135 --> 00:01:21,368
how many objects it will render at once.

36
00:01:22,110 --> 00:01:24,355
On screen is figure two from the paper.

37
00:01:25,098 --> 00:01:27,585
Top left, ten thousand neurons in a single

38
00:01:27,585 --> 00:01:31,205
image. Below it, one region magnified,

39
00:01:31,260 --> 00:01:33,185
with Kaleido's coloring on the left and

40
00:01:33,185 --> 00:01:34,843
random coloring on the right.

41
00:01:35,585 --> 00:01:38,823
Two problems. Load them all, and tell them

42
00:01:38,823 --> 00:01:42,218
apart. The paper has one trick for each.

43
00:01:43,592 --> 00:01:44,950
Trick one is flattening.

44
00:01:45,692 --> 00:01:48,130
Give up on one object per neuron, and draw

45
00:01:48,130 --> 00:01:49,467
every neuron into the same

46
00:01:49,467 --> 00:01:50,875
three-dimensional image.

47
00:01:51,617 --> 00:01:53,655
It is like flattening ten thousand layers

48
00:01:53,655 --> 00:01:56,880
into one picture. Once it is flat, the

49
00:01:56,880 --> 00:01:59,167
file size depends only on the resolution

50
00:01:59,167 --> 00:02:01,367
of that picture, and barely at all on how

51
00:02:01,367 --> 00:02:02,975
many neurons went into it.

52
00:02:03,717 --> 00:02:05,655
The price is that you can no longer handle

53
00:02:05,655 --> 00:02:07,212
a single neuron on its own.

54
00:02:07,955 --> 00:02:09,675
The paper's remedy is this.

55
00:02:10,417 --> 00:02:12,192
When several neurons land on the same

56
00:02:12,192 --> 00:02:14,337
spot, keep only the brightest one.

57
00:02:15,080 --> 00:02:17,580
So every lit pixel belongs to exactly one

58
00:02:17,580 --> 00:02:19,155
neuron, and you can click it

59
00:02:19,155 --> 00:02:20,312
and find out which.

60
00:02:21,700 --> 00:02:23,232
Trick two is the coloring.

61
00:02:23,975 --> 00:02:25,725
Ten thousand neurons have to be told

62
00:02:25,725 --> 00:02:27,933
apart, so they need different colors.

63
00:02:28,675 --> 00:02:30,700
The trouble with random colors is that two

64
00:02:30,700 --> 00:02:32,750
neurons wound around each other can draw

65
00:02:32,750 --> 00:02:35,750
almost the same one. Kaleido turns this

66
00:02:35,750 --> 00:02:37,107
into a physics problem.

67
00:02:37,850 --> 00:02:39,775
Every pair of neurons gets a discomfort

68
00:02:39,775 --> 00:02:42,800
score. The closer they sit, and the more

69
00:02:42,800 --> 00:02:45,245
alike their colors, the higher it goes.

70
00:02:45,987 --> 00:02:47,775
Add up every pair and you have a total

71
00:02:47,775 --> 00:02:51,237
energy. Then Monte Carlo swaps colors over

72
00:02:51,237 --> 00:02:54,200
and over. Accept a swap when the energy

73
00:02:54,200 --> 00:02:56,650
drops, and now and then tolerate one that

74
00:02:56,650 --> 00:02:59,600
makes it worse. It runs until neighbors in

75
00:02:59,600 --> 00:03:01,795
space sit far apart on the hue ring.

76
00:03:02,537 --> 00:03:04,695
The formula holds only two things.

77
00:03:05,438 --> 00:03:07,882
Distance in space, and difference in hue.

78
00:03:09,264 --> 00:03:11,259
The method comes in seven steps.

79
00:03:12,001 --> 00:03:14,134
Move the images into the standard brain.

80
00:03:14,876 --> 00:03:16,901
Take one hundred points from each neuron

81
00:03:16,901 --> 00:03:18,446
and compute the distances.

82
00:03:19,189 --> 00:03:22,151
Pick a color mode. Spread the hue angles

83
00:03:22,151 --> 00:03:24,934
evenly. Run Monte Carlo.

84
00:03:25,676 --> 00:03:27,721
Convert to red, green and blue.

85
00:03:28,464 --> 00:03:31,251
Write the image out. The reproduction

86
00:03:31,251 --> 00:03:32,896
follows those seven steps.

87
00:03:33,639 --> 00:03:35,371
Two more things are worth keeping.

88
00:03:36,114 --> 00:03:39,126
Color can be data. The paper colors the

89
00:03:39,126 --> 00:03:40,789
same neurons by birthdate and by

90
00:03:40,789 --> 00:03:42,976
neurotransmitter, and different patterns

91
00:03:42,976 --> 00:03:45,134
rise out of the same mass of images.

92
00:03:45,876 --> 00:03:47,059
And there is a limit.

93
00:03:47,801 --> 00:03:50,384
The ceiling is optical, not algorithmic.

94
00:03:51,126 --> 00:03:53,659
Good coloring here is defined by geometry,

95
00:03:53,801 --> 00:03:55,821
not by what your eye can really separate.

96
00:03:57,187 --> 00:03:59,425
For the reproduction, the person gave one

97
00:03:59,425 --> 00:04:02,157
prompt. Introduce the paper.

98
00:04:02,900 --> 00:04:05,975
Reproduce the code. Produce three views

99
00:04:05,975 --> 00:04:07,557
and a rotating animation.

100
00:04:08,300 --> 00:04:10,400
Put the code in its own folder, and do not

101
00:04:10,400 --> 00:04:13,337
push it to GitHub. The first thing the AI

102
00:04:13,337 --> 00:04:14,720
did was not write code.

103
00:04:15,462 --> 00:04:17,287
It opened the knowledge-base entry and

104
00:04:17,287 --> 00:04:18,912
copied out the seven steps as its

105
00:04:18,912 --> 00:04:22,175
specification. Then it downloaded the data

106
00:04:22,175 --> 00:04:24,175
and read the first thousand bytes or so of

107
00:04:24,175 --> 00:04:27,557
one file. The header told it four things.

108
00:04:28,300 --> 00:04:31,382
Binary. Sixteen-bit integers.

109
00:04:32,125 --> 00:04:35,287
Compressed. And a bounding box, which is

110
00:04:35,287 --> 00:04:37,287
where this neuron sits inside the standard

111
00:04:37,287 --> 00:04:40,570
brain. That last one matters in a minute.

112
00:04:41,943 --> 00:04:43,525
Next came the file reader.

113
00:04:44,268 --> 00:04:46,555
Seventy-three megabytes per volume times a

114
00:04:46,555 --> 00:04:48,930
hundred neurons will not sit in memory, so

115
00:04:48,930 --> 00:04:50,568
it keeps only the points that are not

116
00:04:50,568 --> 00:04:53,618
empty. It tested the reader on one single

117
00:04:53,618 --> 00:04:56,863
file first. Eight hundredths of a second.

118
00:04:57,605 --> 00:04:59,230
About two hundred and ninety thousand

119
00:04:59,230 --> 00:05:02,555
non-empty points. Values from zero to two

120
00:05:02,555 --> 00:05:03,950
hundred and fifty five.

121
00:05:04,693 --> 00:05:06,755
All three numbers looked right, and only

122
00:05:06,755 --> 00:05:08,063
then did it carry on.

123
00:05:08,805 --> 00:05:11,750
The AI writes fast, so it goes wrong fast.

124
00:05:12,493 --> 00:05:14,305
Leave one output you can look at in every

125
00:05:14,305 --> 00:05:16,488
stage, and a mistake stops there.

126
00:05:17,866 --> 00:05:19,711
Then it wrote the rest in one run.

127
00:05:20,454 --> 00:05:23,761
Distances, energy, Monte Carlo, merging,

128
00:05:23,816 --> 00:05:26,936
three views. It ran the whole pipeline,

129
00:05:26,979 --> 00:05:28,349
and the merge step failed.

130
00:05:29,091 --> 00:05:32,341
An index error. The second axis was two

131
00:05:32,341 --> 00:05:34,779
hundred and sixty nine long, but the index

132
00:05:34,779 --> 00:05:36,099
was two hundred and eighty.

133
00:05:36,841 --> 00:05:38,486
So it checked all the headers.

134
00:05:39,229 --> 00:05:41,091
A hundred files, a hundred different

135
00:05:41,091 --> 00:05:44,141
sizes. Each file is its own cropped

136
00:05:44,141 --> 00:05:46,779
volume. What they really share is the

137
00:05:46,779 --> 00:05:49,004
bounding box, which says where that volume

138
00:05:49,004 --> 00:05:50,549
sits in the standard brain.

139
00:05:51,291 --> 00:05:53,304
So step one of the paper, move into the

140
00:05:53,304 --> 00:05:55,216
standard brain, means something very

141
00:05:55,216 --> 00:05:56,674
specific for this data.

142
00:05:57,416 --> 00:05:59,179
Use the bounding box to put all one

143
00:05:59,179 --> 00:06:01,204
hundred neurons back into one coordinate

144
00:06:01,204 --> 00:06:04,216
system. The first version had assumed they

145
00:06:04,216 --> 00:06:06,574
were the same size, and that was wrong.

146
00:06:07,316 --> 00:06:09,366
But it went wrong fast, and it went wrong

147
00:06:09,366 --> 00:06:10,611
with information in it.

148
00:06:11,354 --> 00:06:13,061
That kind of mistake is fine.

149
00:06:14,430 --> 00:06:16,430
Once they were aligned, the first correct

150
00:06:16,430 --> 00:06:19,205
picture came out. From the front it is

151
00:06:19,205 --> 00:06:21,125
unmistakably a fruit fly brain.

152
00:06:21,867 --> 00:06:24,112
The two round lobes are the optic lobes,

153
00:06:24,142 --> 00:06:26,192
the middle is the central brain, and under

154
00:06:26,192 --> 00:06:28,187
it is the subesophageal ganglion.

155
00:06:28,930 --> 00:06:31,030
Next to it, the top view and the side

156
00:06:31,030 --> 00:06:33,755
view. Whether it looks like a fruit fly

157
00:06:33,755 --> 00:06:35,700
brain is for a person to judge.

158
00:06:36,442 --> 00:06:37,455
Right numbers do not

159
00:06:37,455 --> 00:06:38,975
guarantee a right picture.

160
00:06:40,346 --> 00:06:42,258
Monte Carlo was written the way the paper

161
00:06:42,258 --> 00:06:45,546
describes it. At each step, find the pair

162
00:06:45,546 --> 00:06:47,696
with the highest energy, and swap one of

163
00:06:47,696 --> 00:06:49,541
them with a neuron picked at random.

164
00:06:50,283 --> 00:06:52,246
Twenty thousand steps brought the energy

165
00:06:52,246 --> 00:06:54,246
from about one point five down to one

166
00:06:54,246 --> 00:06:57,158
point two nine. But the curve went flat

167
00:06:57,158 --> 00:06:59,028
after about three thousand steps.

168
00:06:59,771 --> 00:07:01,696
The suspicion was that only ever touching

169
00:07:01,696 --> 00:07:03,746
the worst pair keeps you circling the same

170
00:07:03,746 --> 00:07:06,166
few neurons, stuck in a local minimum.

171
00:07:06,908 --> 00:07:09,408
So it ran a small experiment, mixing in an

172
00:07:09,408 --> 00:07:11,666
equal share of purely random swaps.

173
00:07:12,408 --> 00:07:14,908
The mixed move landed lower, at one point

174
00:07:14,908 --> 00:07:16,928
two six, and it was steadier.

175
00:07:17,671 --> 00:07:19,883
The default changed to mixed, and a switch

176
00:07:19,883 --> 00:07:21,471
was left in to restore the paper's

177
00:07:21,471 --> 00:07:24,716
version. Reproducing is not copying.

178
00:07:25,458 --> 00:07:27,571
But a change needs a switch, and it needs

179
00:07:27,571 --> 00:07:28,641
to be written down.

180
00:07:30,021 --> 00:07:31,696
Then the comparison from the paper's

181
00:07:31,696 --> 00:07:35,046
figure two. The same neurons, with Kaleido

182
00:07:35,046 --> 00:07:37,146
coloring on the left and random coloring

183
00:07:37,146 --> 00:07:40,096
on the right. Honestly, with one hundred

184
00:07:40,096 --> 00:07:42,121
neurons the difference is not as obvious

185
00:07:42,121 --> 00:07:44,159
as the paper's ten thousand, because a

186
00:07:44,159 --> 00:07:45,904
hundred is not crowded enough.

187
00:07:46,646 --> 00:07:48,434
But you can still see it in the magnified

188
00:07:48,434 --> 00:07:51,446
part. On the random side two neighbors

189
00:07:51,446 --> 00:07:53,371
came out almost the same color, and

190
00:07:53,371 --> 00:07:55,209
Kaleido pushes them to green against

191
00:07:55,209 --> 00:07:58,121
magenta. The hue ring on the right makes

192
00:07:58,121 --> 00:07:59,479
the point more directly.

193
00:08:00,221 --> 00:08:02,696
One dot per neuron, and a gray line to its

194
00:08:02,696 --> 00:08:04,266
nearest neighbor in space.

195
00:08:05,009 --> 00:08:06,821
On the Kaleido ring those lines are

196
00:08:06,821 --> 00:08:09,846
clearly longer. When your result is less

197
00:08:09,846 --> 00:08:12,371
pretty than the paper's, say so, and then

198
00:08:12,371 --> 00:08:14,441
find a picture that measures the effect.

199
00:08:15,809 --> 00:08:17,871
The rotating animation is an output the

200
00:08:17,871 --> 00:08:20,821
paper never had. Take the point cloud of a

201
00:08:20,821 --> 00:08:22,684
hundred neurons, turn it around the

202
00:08:22,684 --> 00:08:25,084
vertical axis, and project it at every

203
00:08:25,084 --> 00:08:27,921
angle. The first version came out with a

204
00:08:27,921 --> 00:08:30,596
grid of stripes. Eight hundred and ninety

205
00:08:30,596 --> 00:08:32,659
five voxels of width had to fit into

206
00:08:32,659 --> 00:08:34,154
eleven hundred pixels.

207
00:08:34,896 --> 00:08:36,959
The scaling was not a whole number, so

208
00:08:36,959 --> 00:08:39,184
some columns of pixels caught no points at

209
00:08:39,184 --> 00:08:42,054
all. The program raised no error.

210
00:08:42,796 --> 00:08:44,446
You only find this by looking at the

211
00:08:44,446 --> 00:08:47,509
picture. The second version drew at double

212
00:08:47,509 --> 00:08:49,679
resolution and shrank it back down.

213
00:08:50,421 --> 00:08:52,434
The stripes went away, but the whole thing

214
00:08:52,434 --> 00:08:54,584
got darker, because the empty pixels

215
00:08:54,584 --> 00:08:56,391
between points were averaged in.

216
00:08:57,134 --> 00:08:59,346
The third version fixed one voxel to two

217
00:08:59,346 --> 00:09:01,821
pixels, drew every point as a two by two

218
00:09:01,821 --> 00:09:03,579
block, and then shrank it.

219
00:09:04,321 --> 00:09:06,254
No gaps, normal brightness.

220
00:09:06,996 --> 00:09:08,546
All three versions are written into the

221
00:09:08,546 --> 00:09:09,941
notes inside the code.

222
00:09:11,316 --> 00:09:12,749
This is the finished thing.

223
00:09:13,491 --> 00:09:15,854
One hundred FlyCircuit neurons, colored by

224
00:09:15,854 --> 00:09:18,074
Kaleido, turning one full circle.

225
00:09:18,816 --> 00:09:21,254
The optic lobes are two thick shells, and

226
00:09:21,254 --> 00:09:23,004
in the central brain the neurons layer

227
00:09:23,004 --> 00:09:26,104
front to back. This animation made it into

228
00:09:26,104 --> 00:09:28,166
the video because the video pipeline grew

229
00:09:28,166 --> 00:09:29,449
one new feature for it,

230
00:09:29,516 --> 00:09:30,824
the animated slide.

231
00:09:32,192 --> 00:09:34,112
The repository looks like this.

232
00:09:34,854 --> 00:09:37,217
Five modules, one script that runs the

233
00:09:37,217 --> 00:09:39,504
whole pipeline, one script that makes the

234
00:09:39,504 --> 00:09:41,524
teaching figures, and a read-me.

235
00:09:42,267 --> 00:09:43,799
The read-me holds a table.

236
00:09:44,542 --> 00:09:46,579
Which of the paper's seven steps maps to

237
00:09:46,579 --> 00:09:48,929
which piece of code, what matches, what

238
00:09:48,929 --> 00:09:52,429
does not, and why. Six of the seven steps

239
00:09:52,429 --> 00:09:55,667
were done. Step seven wrote a file for one

240
00:09:55,667 --> 00:09:57,804
particular commercial package, and the

241
00:09:57,804 --> 00:09:59,754
reproduction writes pictures and video

242
00:09:59,754 --> 00:10:01,867
instead, plus an array recording which

243
00:10:01,867 --> 00:10:03,449
neuron owns each voxel.

244
00:10:04,192 --> 00:10:06,174
Things the paper did not have were added.

245
00:10:06,917 --> 00:10:09,417
Three views, the rotation, and that switch

246
00:10:09,417 --> 00:10:12,229
for the move. A finished piece needs a

247
00:10:12,229 --> 00:10:14,867
comparison table, so anyone else can tell

248
00:10:14,867 --> 00:10:16,774
how far this code can be trusted.

249
00:10:18,148 --> 00:10:19,998
Now look back at that stretch and name the

250
00:10:19,998 --> 00:10:23,435
concepts. Vibe coding is Karpathy's phrase

251
00:10:23,435 --> 00:10:25,155
from early twenty twenty five.

252
00:10:25,898 --> 00:10:27,473
You describe what you want in plain

253
00:10:27,473 --> 00:10:30,135
language, the AI generates the code, and

254
00:10:30,135 --> 00:10:31,835
you check the result rather than every

255
00:10:31,835 --> 00:10:34,918
line. Agentic coding goes further.

256
00:10:35,660 --> 00:10:37,580
The AI does not only generate.

257
00:10:38,323 --> 00:10:40,723
It plans the steps, runs them, looks at

258
00:10:40,723 --> 00:10:43,610
the output, fixes things, runs again, and

259
00:10:43,610 --> 00:10:45,030
reports when it is done.

260
00:10:45,773 --> 00:10:47,730
This episode is the second kind.

261
00:10:48,473 --> 00:10:50,760
A person gave one prompt, and the AI

262
00:10:50,760 --> 00:10:51,998
walked the twelve steps in

263
00:10:51,998 --> 00:10:53,155
between on its own.

264
00:10:54,527 --> 00:10:56,835
Here is the division of labour, laid out.

265
00:10:57,577 --> 00:11:00,815
The person did this. One prompt, with the

266
00:11:00,815 --> 00:11:02,860
deliverables and the boundaries in it.

267
00:11:03,602 --> 00:11:04,872
Handed over the data.

268
00:11:05,615 --> 00:11:07,360
And looked at the result at the end.

269
00:11:08,102 --> 00:11:11,265
The AI did the rest. Broke the job into a

270
00:11:11,265 --> 00:11:13,727
list, read the specification, read the

271
00:11:13,727 --> 00:11:16,315
file headers, wrote the code, tested every

272
00:11:16,315 --> 00:11:19,147
piece small, hit walls, diagnosed them,

273
00:11:19,202 --> 00:11:21,622
ran an experiment, wrote three versions,

274
00:11:21,677 --> 00:11:22,835
and wrote the read-me.

275
00:11:23,577 --> 00:11:25,702
What separates this from asking an AI to

276
00:11:25,702 --> 00:11:27,535
write you one function is the loop.

277
00:11:28,277 --> 00:11:31,352
It writes, runs, looks, fixes, and keeps

278
00:11:31,352 --> 00:11:33,072
going until it stops moving.

279
00:11:33,815 --> 00:11:36,110
Here that loop turned at least six times,

280
00:11:36,177 --> 00:11:38,115
and every judgement rested on output it

281
00:11:38,115 --> 00:11:39,422
had produced itself.

282
00:11:40,795 --> 00:11:42,252
So where does it go wrong?

283
00:11:42,995 --> 00:11:46,520
Four places. First, things that look right

284
00:11:46,520 --> 00:11:49,370
and are not. The moiré version raised no

285
00:11:49,370 --> 00:11:52,295
error at all. The AI caught it because it

286
00:11:52,295 --> 00:11:53,902
drew the picture and looked at it.

287
00:11:54,645 --> 00:11:56,970
Second, changing the specification on its

288
00:11:56,970 --> 00:11:59,882
own. Change the move and it is no longer a

289
00:11:59,882 --> 00:12:02,082
reproduction, so leave a switch and write

290
00:12:02,082 --> 00:12:05,390
it down. Third, being too cautious.

291
00:12:06,132 --> 00:12:08,595
Last episode the AI left out every figure

292
00:12:08,595 --> 00:12:10,545
because of a copyright note, and it was

293
00:12:10,545 --> 00:12:13,557
sent back. Judging that boundary belongs

294
00:12:13,557 --> 00:12:16,732
to a person. Fourth, being honest when the

295
00:12:16,732 --> 00:12:19,557
result disappoints. The hundred-neuron

296
00:12:19,557 --> 00:12:21,570
comparison is less dramatic than the

297
00:12:21,570 --> 00:12:25,220
paper's. Say it. Do not talk

298
00:12:25,220 --> 00:12:26,277
your way around it.

299
00:12:27,662 --> 00:12:29,525
Four suggestions if you want to work this

300
00:12:29,525 --> 00:12:32,537
way. One, name the deliverables in your

301
00:12:32,537 --> 00:12:36,232
prompt. Three views, a rotating animation,

302
00:12:36,275 --> 00:12:38,532
where the code goes, no push to GitHub.

303
00:12:39,275 --> 00:12:40,870
Those are all deliverables.

304
00:12:41,612 --> 00:12:43,637
Two, have it build the smallest thing you

305
00:12:43,637 --> 00:12:46,295
can check, look at that, then scale up.

306
00:12:47,037 --> 00:12:49,162
Three, make it write the walls it hit and

307
00:12:49,162 --> 00:12:50,875
the calls it made into the code and the

308
00:12:50,875 --> 00:12:52,982
read-me, not just into the chat.

309
00:12:53,725 --> 00:12:55,657
Four, you check the result.

310
00:12:56,400 --> 00:12:58,500
Close enough, good enough, should this

311
00:12:58,500 --> 00:13:01,795
figure go in. Those calls are still yours.

312
00:13:03,178 --> 00:13:04,973
Three things this episode covered.

313
00:13:05,715 --> 00:13:07,198
The paper's two tricks.

314
00:13:07,940 --> 00:13:09,990
Flattening the layers cuts memory loose

315
00:13:09,990 --> 00:13:11,903
from the number of neurons, and writing

316
00:13:11,903 --> 00:13:14,003
the coloring as an energy function lets

317
00:13:14,003 --> 00:13:15,635
Monte Carlo minimise it.

318
00:13:16,378 --> 00:13:17,985
What the reproduction taught.

319
00:13:18,728 --> 00:13:20,503
A step that takes one sentence in the

320
00:13:20,503 --> 00:13:22,303
paper can be the whole problem on real

321
00:13:22,303 --> 00:13:24,353
data, and the loop needs output you can

322
00:13:24,353 --> 00:13:27,173
look at. And the division of labour.

323
00:13:27,915 --> 00:13:30,090
Specification and boundaries belong to the

324
00:13:30,090 --> 00:13:33,348
person. The loop belongs to the AI.

325
00:13:34,734 --> 00:13:37,216
If you remember one thing, remember this.

326
00:13:37,959 --> 00:13:40,046
The quality of agentic coding depends on

327
00:13:40,046 --> 00:13:41,684
whether the loop produces something you

328
00:13:41,684 --> 00:13:44,471
can look at. Make every step produce

329
00:13:44,471 --> 00:13:47,679
something checkable. A number, a picture,

330
00:13:47,696 --> 00:13:50,859
an error message. Then the AI can correct

331
00:13:50,859 --> 00:13:53,521
itself. Without that, it is

332
00:13:53,521 --> 00:13:54,916
only writing fast.

333
00:13:56,281 --> 00:13:58,056
The repository has not been pushed to

334
00:13:58,056 --> 00:14:00,956
GitHub yet. And the knowledge base holds

335
00:14:00,956 --> 00:14:02,981
other tool papers that could be reproduced

336
00:14:02,981 --> 00:14:05,851
the same way. See you next time.
